  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure will continue to dominate the weather pattern for\nthe region through Saturday morning. Expect abundant sunshine,\nabove normal temperature that peaks Saturday afternoon, and dry\nconditions.\n\nRain may creep into southern zones by Saturday evening along\nwith increasing cloud cover, but the bulk of Hurricane Isaias's\nremnants arrive Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nHurricane Isaias will weaken after landfall while working\ngenerally NNE underneath the prevailing ridge Saturday into\nSunday, with fairly high confidence the initial precipitation\nshield reaches northern WV/southeastern OH in the pre-dawn hours\nSunday. The combination of warm, moist air (highlighted by\n90th-95th percentile PWATS) and residual trough dynamics favors\nfairly widespread, all-day rain that sees narrow enhancements to \nprecipitation totals. Ensembles remain consistent in a quick \nexit east Sunday night that sees lingering light rain/sprinkles \ncoming to a stop Monday morning.\n\nThe large cloud shield associated with Isaias's remnants\nstrongly favors a minimization of instability (less than 100\nJ/kg SBCAPE) that ensures severe hazards are not existent.\nImpact focus then is on rainfall accumulation totals and\nsynoptic wind gust potential. For flood concerns, ensembles \nstill suggest scenarios where localized rainfall totals enter\nthe 2-3\" range (versus more consensus 1-1.5\") that could be just\nenough to create localized flooding in poor drainage or flood\nsusceptible areas; specifically, the increase of falling leaf \ndebris in storm drains could limit effectiveness of planned \nmitigation efforts and aid in the creation of roadway ponding.\nThe prolonged drier stretch and lack in stronger buoyancy,\nhowever, should keep rates low enough to keep risks low. For\nwind concerns, pending 850mb/700mb depth/positioning, there is\npotential for a low-level easterly jet that creates a\ndownsloping effect east of Pittsburgh. Gusts areawide between\n15-30mph will be common but initial ensembles suggests a 10%\nprobability for 50+ mph gusts sometime Sunday for portions of\nTucker County as well as near Latrobe. Monitoring of these\nconcerns will continue, especially when higher-resolution \nmodeling starts reaching these forecast hours.",
